course: biology
lesson: 140
title: Paw Patrol: real police, fire, rescue and sniffer dogs
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: The Paw Patrol pups drive diggers and fly helicopters, which no dog can do, but real dogs do have rescue jobs: they track people, sniff out hidden things, swim to swimmers and dig through snow.
explain: Their secret weapon is the nose: a dog has hundreds of millions of smell cells, sniffs five to ten times a second and can smell a person buried under a metre of snow.
explain: Picture an airport: a dog walks calmly past a row of suitcases, then sits beside one, because it smelled the thing it was trained to find and knows its favourite toy is coming.
explain: Real working dogs do not obey at once by magic; they learn through months of training with toys, treats and praise, and to many of them the job is a great game.
fact: Police forces use dogs, very often German shepherds and Belgian Malinois, to track people, search buildings and find hidden things, because these breeds are strong, quick to learn and good at following a scent.
fact: A police dog lives with its handler and usually retires to live with the handler's family as a pet.
fact: Detection dogs are trained to sniff out things such as drugs, explosives, fruit carried in bags, money and even some illnesses.
fact: A dog's nose has hundreds of millions of smell cells, the smell part of its brain is huge compared with ours, it sniffs about five to ten times a second, and its nose keeps smelling air apart from breathing air.
fact: Dalmatians really were fire dogs that ran beside horse-drawn fire carts, clearing the way and keeping the horses calm, and some American fire stations still keep one as a mascot.
fact: Dalmatian puppies are born white, their spots appear after about two weeks and keep filling in for months, and many Dalmatians are deaf in one or both ears, linked to the genes that make them so white.
fact: Labradors and Newfoundlands are water rescue dogs with water-resistant coats, webbed toes and strong swimming muscles; in Italy trained dogs jump from helicopters to help swimmers, and a Newfoundland can tow a small boat.
fact: Huskies are sled dogs with thick double coats and furry feet that can run for many hours in the cold, and a husky curls up in snow with its fluffy tail over its nose.
fact: Avalanche dogs find people buried in snow by smell rising through gaps in the snow, searching in minutes an area that would take a team of people hours.
fact: Real bulldogs are not builders or strong swimmers; they were bred long ago for a cruel sport and later for looks, which gave them short faces and heavy bodies that make hard work difficult.
fact: No dog can fly, but small light dogs help in rescues by wriggling into gaps and over unstable rubble, and some dogs are lowered from rescue helicopters on ropes with their handlers.
fact: Working dogs learn over months with rewards like toys, treats and praise, with big jobs broken into small steps, and many think of the job as a game that ends with their favourite toy.
fact: Dogs understand human pointing better than chimpanzees and wolves do, after thousands of years living with people, and they often look at the left side of a person's face.
exercise: Which breeds are very often used as police dogs? | German shepherds and Belgian Malinois | Chihuahuas and pugs, because they are small and quick | Bulldogs and dachshunds, because they are strong | Poodles and corgis, because they are clever
exercise: Where does a police dog usually live when it retires? | With its handler's family as a pet | In a dog prison | Alone in a kennel at the police station, locked up at night | In the wild with wolves
exercise: Which of these can detection dogs be trained to find? | Drugs, explosives, fruit in bags, money and some illnesses | Only lost tennis balls | Only bones that other dogs have buried deep in people's gardens | Only cats hiding in trees
exercise: About how often does a dog sniff when searching? | Five to ten times a second | Once a minute, very deeply | Once an hour | A hundred times a second
exercise: What did Dalmatians do with old fire carts? | Ran beside them, cleared the way and calmed the horses | Pulled the heavy fire cart all by themselves, with no horses | Sprayed water on the fire | Rang the fire bell with their paws
exercise: When do a Dalmatian puppy's spots appear? | After about two weeks | Before it is even born | When it is ten years old | Never, the spots are painted on
exercise: What problem is linked to the genes that make Dalmatians so white? | Many are deaf in one or both ears | Many cannot run fast or far | Many have no tail | Many cannot see colours at all
exercise: What helps a Labrador or Newfoundland rescue swimmers? | Water-resistant coats, webbed toes and strong swimming muscles | Gills hidden behind their ears, so they can breathe under the water | Fins on their backs | Floating bones full of air
exercise: What do trained rescue dogs do in Italy? | Jump from helicopters to help swimmers | Drive the lifeboats out to sea by themselves | Fly little rescue planes out to the beach | Guard the ice cream shops along the beach
exercise: How does a husky keep warm when it sleeps in snow? | Curls up with its fluffy tail over its nose | Digs down through the snow to a hot spring | Shivers all night long | Sleeps standing on three legs
exercise: How does an avalanche dog find a buried person? | It smells scent rising through gaps in the snow | It hears the buried person's heartbeat under the snow | It sees straight through the snow with its sharp eyes | It feels the person's warmth through the snow with its paws
exercise: Why are real bulldogs poor at hard work and swimming? | Their short faces and heavy bodies make it difficult | They are too clever to bother swimming when they could walk | Their legs are too long | They hate the smell of water
exercise: Why are small light dogs useful at a building collapse? | They wriggle into gaps without making rubble collapse more | They can lift heavy stones and bricks with their strong little jaws | They can fly over the rubble | They bark louder than big dogs
exercise: Can any dog fly a helicopter? | No, but some dogs are lowered from rescue helicopters with handlers | Yes, small dogs can fly little helicopters if they are trained for years | Yes, with special training | Only in Italy
exercise: How do real working dogs learn their jobs? | Months of training with toys, treats and praise | They are born knowing them, so they need no lessons at all | They learn it all by watching rescue cartoons on TV | They are told once and remember it for the rest of their lives
exercise: How do many working dogs see their job? | As a game that ends with their favourite toy | As a punishment | As something they hate but must do, or they get no dinner | As a way to get away from people
exercise: Who understands human pointing better? | Dogs, better than chimpanzees and wolves | Wolves, better than dogs, as they are cleverer | Chimpanzees, much better than dogs and wolves | Cats, better than dogs, wolves or chimpanzees
test: «newborn Dalmatian litter, 8 pups, how many spots altogether lol» | None, they are born white | 8, one each | About 800 | 16, two each
test: «Paw Patrol's 6 pups lined up, how many ears is that» | 12 | 6 | 24 | 18
test: In 101 Dalmatians the puppies already have their spots as tiny babies. What is real? | Dalmatian puppies are born white and the spots come after about two weeks | Dalmatians are born with all their spots, which just get bigger as they grow | Dalmatians are born black and turn white | Dalmatian spots are painted by breeders
test: In Tom and Jerry, Spike the bulldog guards the yard and swims across the pool to catch Tom. What is true of real bulldogs? | They are poor swimmers because of their heavy fronts and short noses | Bulldogs are the best swimming dogs, which is why lifeguards use them at the beach | Bulldogs were bred as lifeguards | Bulldogs float like ducks
test: «Ryder says go and they just go, real dogs don't need training» What is right? | Real working dogs need months of training with rewards | Yes, every dog knows how to do rescue work from the day it is born | Yes, dogs understand every word | No, dogs can never be trained
test: «my mate reckons dogs can fly helicopters like Skye» What is right? | No dog can fly one; some dogs ride in rescue helicopters with handlers | Yes, small dogs fly them in rescues, after a few years of training with a pilot | Yes, but only in Italy | No, dogs are scared of helicopters
test: «dog at the airport sat down next to my nan's suitcase, why» What does that usually mean? | It smelled something it was trained to find, maybe fruit she packed | It was tired after a long walk and needed to lie down for a rest | It wanted to go on holiday | It liked the colour of the case
test: «avalanche, dude's under the snow, can't we just poke around with sticks» Why is a dog better? | It smells scent rising through the snow and searches in minutes what takes people hours | Dogs can melt the snow with their warm breath and dig people out much faster than any stick | Dogs can see through snow | Sticks always miss people
test: What would happen if an avalanche dog had a bad cold and blocked nose? | It would find buried people much less well, since it searches by smell | It would find them faster by sight, as dogs can see through snow on clear days | Nothing, it searches by hearing | It would find them by tasting the snow
test: «you point at the ball and the dog looks at your finger, dogs are thick» What is right? | Dogs follow pointing better than chimpanzees and wolves do | Right, dogs never understand pointing and only look at your finger | Only wolves understand pointing | Dogs only look at your feet
test: «why do working dogs go so mad for their toy at the end» What is the reason? | Finding the target means play with their favourite toy, so the job feels like a game | They are hungry for the toy, because working dogs are fed on toys instead of food | The toy has food inside | They are scared of the toy
test: «Dalmatian at the fire station, just looks cool or what» Why are they linked to fire stations? | They once ran beside horse-drawn fire carts, clearing the way and calming the horses | They can smell fire long before people can, so they ran to warn the firemen | They are not afraid of flames | They were bred to put out fires
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the coat of the Dalmatian at birth. | The puppy is born white and the spots appear after approximately two weeks | The puppy is born with its full pattern of spots, which merely enlarge with growth | The puppy is born black and subsequently turns white | The spots are applied artificially by breeders
exam: Identify the principal faculty by which avalanche dogs locate persons buried beneath snow. | The sense of smell, which detects scent rising through the snow | The sense of sight, which penetrates the snow | The sense of hearing, which detects the heartbeat | The sense of touch, which detects warmth through the paws
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the historical function of the Dalmatian in respect of fire services? | It ran beside horse-drawn fire carts, clearing the way and calming the horses | It extinguished fires by means of its trained behaviour | It pulled the fire carts without the assistance of horses, being harnessed in their place | It detected fires by the scent of smoke at great distances
exam: Ascertain the method by which working dogs acquire their skills. | Training over several months by means of rewards such as toys, food and praise | Instinct present from birth without any instruction | A single spoken instruction which is retained permanently | Observation of the work of other species, such as horses, over a period of several years
